"The surah addresses worldly wealth and the delusion of abundance that distracts people from their true purpose. The word 'alha' means distraction, occupying oneself with futile things and being diverted from important matters. 'Takathur', on the other hand, means boasting of abundance and competing for superiority against one another.\n\nThis boasting occurs through worldly things such as wealth, children, lineage, or tribal numbers. The verse is stated unconditionally; therefore, it encompasses boasting of every kind of abundance. However, what is specifically intended is boasting of worldly matters that divert a person from obedience to Allah.\n\nThere are different narrations regarding the reason for revelation: According to one narration, the Banu Abd Manaf and Banu Sahm, and according to another, two Ansar tribes of Madinah, competed over which was more numerous by counting even their dead. In another narration, greed for wealth is emphasized.\n\nThree different meanings have been given to the expression 'until you visit the graves' in the verse: First, boasting of the number of the dead and physically visiting the graves; second, remaining in this heedlessness until the moment of death and awakening with death; and third, tasting the punishment of the grave.\n\nThe verse does not condemn boasting of abundance in an absolute sense, but rather taking pride in worldly wealth that diverts one from servitude to Allah. Competing in good deeds and taking pride in righteous actions is praised. What matters is sincerity and devotion (ikhlas).\n\nVisiting graves is permissible and even a Sunnah if done to take heed and remember the Hereafter. However, visits made for the purpose of boasting and pride are condemned. Our Prophet first forbade visiting graves, and later permitted it for the sake of taking heed.\n\nIn summary, the surah warns people against being preoccupied with worldly wealth and the delusion of abundance, and reminds them of the Hereafter. When visiting graves, the primary purpose must be to take heed and awaken from heedlessness.\n\nIn this section, it is stated that boasting of abundance distracts a person until they reach the graves. Visiting graves was actually made permissible to take heed and remember the Hereafter. However, some have abused this visit to boast of the great number of their dead, and to take pride by using their names and fame. Such behaviors deserve the condemnation and rebuke of the verse. It is not correct to deny the lawful aspect of visiting graves; extensive information on this subject is found in books of jurisprudence (fiqh).\n\nAccording to books of jurisprudence, visiting graves is permissible, and even a Sunnah. In works such as al-Durr al-Mukhtar, Imdad, al-Mujtaba, al-Bahr, and Radd al-Muhtar, it is explained that there is no objection even for women, through the Prophet's hadith: 'I had forbidden you to visit graves, so go and visit them.' The most virtuous days for visiting are Friday, Saturday, Sunday, Monday, and Thursday. It is Sunnah to supplicate while standing during the visit; when the Prophet went out to Baqi', he would say: 'Peace be upon you, O inhabitants of the abode of believers! We, if Allah wills, shall join you.' Furthermore, the Quran may be recited at the head or feet of the deceased; reciting al-Fatihah, the beginning of Surah al-Baqarah, Ayat al-Kursi, Amanar-Rasul, Ya-Sin, al-Mulk, Surah at-Takathur, and Surah al-Ikhlas is recommended.\n\nIn Ibn Abidin's Radd al-Muhtar, it is stated that gifting reward to the deceased is permissible, and that the rewards of deeds such as prayer, fasting, and charity can be bestowed upon others. In fact, in charity, intending for all believing men and women is more virtuous. Although some scholars wished to forbid gifting the reward of reciting the Quran to the Prophet, the majority argued that there was no need for such a restriction.\n\nIn his bequest to Abu Yusuf, Imam al-A'zam advises that in the face of the ruler's conduct contrary to knowledge, one should remind him of the truth while obeying, debate if he is an adherent of innovation, and take refuge in Allah if he does not accept. He also counsels visiting graves, shaykhs, and blessed places. All of this shows the legitimacy and wisdom of visiting graves; the essence is not to ask for help from the dead, but to give greetings of peace, pray to Allah, and gift rewards.\n\nThe concept of 'yaqin' (certain knowledge) mentioned in the verse is emphasized. Yaqin is knowledge free of doubt. It has three degrees: 'ilm al-yaqin (knowledge through reason and evidence), 'ayn al-yaqin (knowledge through seeing with the eye), and haqq al-yaqin (knowledge through direct personal experience). The expression 'ilma'l-yaqin' in this verse means either certain knowledge, or what is known in an absolute sense, or knowledge of death. Commentators are in agreement that the response to 'in' has been elided; it is estimated either as 'had you known, you would not have done so' or as 'what you would have done cannot be described.' The second view is preferred.\n\nThis verse contains a grave warning for scholars. For those who know with certainty the calamity at the end of boasting of abundance desist from this conduct. Thus, there is no certain knowledge in those who do not abandon boasting. The condition of those who bear the title of scholar yet do not act upon their knowledge is dire. Knowledge must be the most powerful motivator for righteous deeds. Otherwise, on the Day of Resurrection, there will be grief and regret.\n\nIn the next verse, it is declared: 'By all means, you shall surely see Hellfire.' This statement is not the response to the preceding 'in', but an independent threat in its own right. The 'lam' is for an oath. Hell is a blazing fire. Commentators state that this verse is not the response to 'in', because 'in' expresses impossibility; here, the aim is to inform that Hell will certainly be seen in the future. Although some have estimated it as 'had you known, you would have seen it,' this is more convoluted.\n\nThen, it is declared: 'Then you shall surely see it with the eye of certainty.' 'Ayn' means either the eye, that is, seeing clearly; or essence, meaning certainty itself. The second meaning points to the degree of haqq al-yaqin; and this occurs by entering Hell. According to this interpretation, the first seeing is at the Gathering, and the second seeing is after entering Hell.\n\nIn the final verse, it is declared: 'Then, on that Day, you shall surely be questioned about the blessings.' 'Na'im' encompasses every kind of blessing: life, health, well-being, even a single sip of water. The address is directed at those who are distracted by their worldly life; and the questioning is by way of rebuke and punishment for those who misuse these blessings. Some commentators have said that the questioning applies only to disbelievers, while others have stated that it encompasses everyone, both believer and disbeliever. Narrations indicate that this questioning is general. Indeed, the Prophet (peace be upon him) informed us that we will be questioned about blessings, even if it be a single date and water.\n\nIt is well known that the Prophet (peace be upon him) and his Companions avoided ostentation to the utmost in their livelihood and spent blessings in the way of Allah. Accountability is proportional to the abundance of blessings. Therefore, boasting of abundance is a great heedlessness. The expression 'except for those who believe and do righteous deeds' in Surah al-Asr serves as an explanation of this verse."